.TH HSH 1 "May 2026" "hsh 1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
hsh \- simple UNIX command interpreter
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B hsh
.br
.B ./hsh
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B hsh
is a simple UNIX command language interpreter that executes commands read from
standard input or from a file. It is a stripped-down reimplementation of
.BR sh (1)
that supports command execution with arguments and resolves program names
against the
.B PATH
environment variable.
.PP
When invoked with no arguments and standard input is a terminal,
.B hsh
runs in
.B interactive
mode: it displays a prompt and reads commands one line at a time. When
standard input is not a terminal (e.g. a pipe or a redirected file),
.B hsh
runs in
.B non-interactive
mode: it reads and executes commands without displaying a prompt, then exits
on end-of-file.
.SH INVOCATION
.B hsh
takes no command-line options. The shell is started simply by running its
executable:
.PP
.RS 4
./hsh
.RE
.SH COMMAND EXECUTION
After reading a line of input,
.B hsh
splits it into tokens on whitespace. The first token is treated as the
command name; any remaining tokens are passed as arguments.
.PP
If the command name contains a slash
.RB ( / ),
it is treated as a path and executed directly. Otherwise,
.B hsh
searches each directory listed in the
.B PATH
environment variable, in order, for an executable file of that name. The
first match is executed via
.BR execve (2)
in a forked child process; the parent waits for the child to terminate
before reading the next command.
.PP
If the command cannot be found in any
.B PATH
directory,
.B hsh
prints an error to standard error and does not fork a new process.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.B hsh
returns the exit status of the last command executed. If a command is not
found, the shell prints an error and the exit status is set to
.BR 127 .
On end-of-file (Ctrl-D in interactive mode),
.B hsh
exits with status
.BR 0 .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B PATH
A colon-separated list of directories in which the shell looks for commands.
If
.B PATH
is unset or empty, no PATH search is performed and only commands given as
explicit paths can be executed.
.SH EXAMPLES
.PP
Interactive use:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ ./hsh
($) /bin/ls
file1  file2  hsh
($) ls -l /tmp
total 0
($)
$
.fi
.RE
.PP
Non-interactive use:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ echo "/bin/ls" | ./hsh
file1  file2  hsh

$ cat commands.txt | ./hsh
.fi
.RE
.PP
Command not found:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ echo "qwerty" | ./hsh
./hsh: 1: qwerty: not found
.fi
.RE
.SH LIMITATIONS
.B hsh
is intentionally minimal. It does not currently support:
.IP \[bu] 2
Built-in commands such as
.B cd
or
.BR exit .
.IP \[bu] 2
Pipelines
.RB ( | )
or I/O redirection
.RB ( > ", " < ).
.IP \[bu] 2
Variable expansion or quoting.
.IP \[bu] 2
Background execution
.RB ( & ).
.IP \[bu] 2
Command history or line editing.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR sh (1),
.BR bash (1),
.BR execve (2),
.BR fork (2),
.BR wait (2),
.BR access (2)
